{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And it was told Abraham, saying: Behold, Milcah too has borne.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, explained this tidings thus: Abraham was informed about his elder brother, who was remembered with many children, in order to make known that Nahor and Milcah were old and had not been remembered in their youth, and now in their old age a miracle was wrought for them and she gave birth by way of miracle for the sake of Sarah her sister — and this is the sense of 'Milcah too' — end of quote. And what will the Rabbi say concerning his concubine? For if a miracle was performed for Milcah the wife of Nahor in honor of Abraham, what need was there for his concubine, whom Scripture mentions: 'and she too bore'? And Scripture made known that there were eight, corresponding to the sons of Jacob who were eight sons of the mistresses, and four sons to his concubine Reumah, corresponding to the sons of Jacob who were four sons of the maidservants; and Scripture equated the family of Nahor to the family of Abraham. And the mention of all these genealogies and the narration in the pericope is in order to call attention to Rebekah, and to make known that Rebekah, from whom Jacob issued, was of the house of Abraham's father and his family. And all this is to clarify that the sons of Nahor are fitting to be joined to the seed of Abraham, those who cleave to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 4): ״ואתם הדבקים בה׳ אלהיכם חיים כלכם היום״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:19.091441800Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}